Hmm, but wait - is that true? In C, yes: "If there are fewer initializers in a brace-enclosed list than there are elements or members of an aggregate, or fewer characters in a string literal used to initialize an array of known size than there are elements in the array, the remainder of the aggregate shall be initialized implicitly the same as objects that have static storage duration."

So `struct match match = MATCH_CATCHALL_INITIALIZER` should zero-initialize everything. But wait - there's a subtle issue here. The match struct might have padding bytes that are not zeroed. No, the standard says all of it gets zero-initialized.

